Sec. 80001 amends the asset-exemption rules in Section 480(f)(2) of the Higher Education Act so that, in addition to the existing exclusion, a family farm on which the family resides, a family-owned small business with no more than 100 full-time or full-time-equivalent employees, and a family-owned commercial fishing business (including vessels and permits) are excluded from the "net value" calculation. These changes take effect July 1, 2026, and apply to award year 2026-2027 and each subsequent award year.

Sec. 81001 amends Section 455(a) of the Higher Education Act to terminate graduate/professional students' eligibility for Federal Direct PLUS Loans and to set new loan limits beginning July 1, 2026: $20,500/year for graduate students and $50,000/year for professional students in Direct Unsubsidized Stafford Loans, with aggregate caps of $100,000 (graduate) and $200,000 (professional), a $257,500 lifetime aggregate cap per student, and parent PLUS limits of $20,000/year and $65,000 aggregate per dependent student. It also provides an interim exception for certain students enrolled as of June 30, 2026, and allows institutions to set lower limits.

Sec. 82001 transitions borrowers in income-contingent repayment plans to income-based plans by July 1, 2028, repeals the income-contingent repayment authority effective July 1, 2028, and creates a new Repayment Assistance Plan under Section 455(q) beginning July 1, 2026, with payments based on adjusted gross income (ranging from $120 to 10 percent of AGI), a $10 minimum, interest subsidies, and $50 matching principal payments for distressed borrowers. It also bars loans made on or after July 1, 2026, from using repayment plans other than a standard plan (10-25 years based on principal) or the Repayment Assistance Plan, and requires excepted loans (e.g., parent PLUS) to be repaid under the standard plan.

Sec. 82002 sunsets unemployment and economic hardship deferments for loans made on or after July 1, 2027, and limits forbearance for such loans to no more than 9 months during any 24-month period.

Sec. 82003 allows loan rehabilitation two times (instead of once) for FFEL/Direct loans and Perkins loans, effective July 1, 2027, and sets a minimum total monthly rehabilitation payment of $10 for borrowers with Direct loans made on or after July 1, 2027.

Sec. 82004 amends the Public Service Loan Forgiveness eligibility rules in Section 455(m)(1)(A) to count on-time payments under the new Repayment Assistance Plan under subsection (q) as qualifying payments.

Sec. 82005 amends Section 458(a)(1) of the Higher Education Act to provide $1,000,000,000 in mandatory funds to the Secretary of Education for administrative costs, including servicing of the Direct Student Loan programs, available until expended.

Sec. 83001 modifies the definition of "adjusted gross income" for Pell Grant eligibility to include parents' or students' foreign income for award years beginning July 1, 2026, sunsets the existing foreign-income exclusion for years before July 1, 2026, and makes students ineligible for Pell Grants if their student aid index equals or exceeds twice the maximum Pell Grant, effective July 1, 2026.

Sec. 83002 establishes the Workforce Pell Grant program under new Section 401(k), effective July 1, 2026, for students enrolled in eligible short-term workforce programs of at least 150 but less than 600 clock hours over 8 to 15 weeks, with state governor determinations and Secretary approval based on completion rates of at least 70 percent, job placement rates of at least 70 percent, and tuition not exceeding value-added earnings.

Sec. 83003 increases the Pell Grant shortfall appropriation in Section 401(b)(7)(A)(iii) from $2,170,000,000 to $12,670,000,000.

Sec. 83004 makes students ineligible for Federal Pell Grants beginning July 1, 2026, during any period in which they receive non-Federal grant aid (from states, institutions, or private sources) equal to or exceeding their cost of attendance.

Sec. 84001 amends Section 454 of the Higher Education Act to bar institutions, beginning July 1, 2026, from using Title IV funds for enrollment in programs whose median graduate earnings are below the median earnings of working adults (ages 25-34 with only a high school diploma, or a baccalaureate for graduate programs) for at least 2 of the 3 preceding years, with provisions for small cohorts (fewer than 30 students), an appeals process, student notice requirements, and a process to regain eligibility after at least 2 years.

Sec. 85001 delays the November 1, 2022 borrower defense to repayment regulations for loans originating before July 1, 2035, and restores the regulations in effect as of July 1, 2020.

Sec. 85002 delays the November 1, 2022 closed school discharge regulations for loans originating before July 1, 2035, and restores the prior regulations as if the 2022 amendments had not been made.

Sec. 86001 appropriates $40,000,000 to the National Endowment for the Humanities for fiscal year 2025 (available through fiscal year 2028) for procuring statues as described in Executive Orders 13934, 13978, and 14189 relating to the National Garden of American Heroes.

Sec. 87001 appropriates $300,000,000 to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for fiscal year 2025 (available until September 30, 2028) for background checks and home studies of potential sponsors of unaccompanied alien children, gang-tattoo examinations, data systems improvement, and coordination with state child welfare agencies. [end of text]


